The AI wasn't the only thing that was broken. There are holes in
game design, too, that you have to avoid if you want to have any
fun.  The sequel needs to watch out for them.

Here's a brief list off the top of my head.  They mostly reside
in the custom wizard function.

  If you take a full rack of books of a single magic color, you
  can select a starting spell that allows you to win easily. The
  best example of this is Black and the Summon Wraiths spell.

  Alchemy+Artificer+(I forget) gives you an unlimited
  mana-battery by allowing you to break artifacts for twice the
  mana you invested in making them.
  
  Unbalanced Wizard Skills, notably:  Warlord, Alchemy, Myrran.

  Worthless Wizard Skills.  I have trouble remembering what they
  are, since I never used them, e.g., Life Mastery.

  Paladins. The unit with nearly no weaknesses (see next item).

  Web+Cracks Call. There's only one defense to this devestating
  combination.

  The Uber-Army. You're required to build one unkillable stack
  that can go everywhere fast and kill everything. Once you do
  so, you win. A balanced armada of powerful armies is
  impracticle.
  
  Basic units are not cost effective.

  The economy builds too slowly, and suddenly surges to
  ridiculous.

  Terrain figures too prominently.  Certain mines mean easy
  victory.  Lack of rivers means poverty.

  Most City Spells are too strong.

